Here's the recursive binary search translated into JavaScript:

```javascript
function binarySearch(arr, target, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
    if (left > right) return -1;
    const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
    if (arr[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (arr[mid] < target) return binarySearch(arr, target, mid + 1, right);
    return binarySearch(arr, target, left, mid - 1);
}
```

The logic is identical to the Python recursive version. Key adaptations to JavaScript conventions include: using `function` declaration syntax, `const` for the immutable `mid` value, `Math.floor` instead of `//` for integer division, and `===` for strict equality. The default parameter for `right` is set directly to `arr.length - 1` since JavaScript default parameters can reference earlier parameters.

